DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       mmkkee22ffss is used to create an ext2, ext3, or ext4 file system, usually in
       a disk partition (or file) named by _d_e_v_i_c_e.

       The file system size is specified by _f_s_-_s_i_z_e.  If _f_s_-_s_i_z_e does not have
       a  suffix,  it  is interpreted as power-of-two kilobytes, unless the --bb
       _b_l_o_c_k_s_i_z_e option is specified, in which case _f_s_-_s_i_z_e is interpreted  as
       the  number  of  _b_l_o_c_k_s_i_z_e blocks.   If the fs-size is suffixed by 'k',
       'm', 'g', 't' (either upper-case or lower-case), then it is interpreted
       in  power-of-two  kilobytes,  megabytes, gigabytes, terabytes, etc.  If
       _f_s_-_s_i_z_e is omitted, mmkkee22ffss will create the file system based on the de‐
       vice size.

       If mmkkee22ffss is run as mmkkffss..XXXXXX (i.e., mmkkffss..eexxtt22, mmkkffss..eexxtt33, or mmkkffss..eexxtt44)
       the option --tt _X_X_X is implied; so mmkkffss..eexxtt33 will create  a  file  system
       for  use  with  ext3,  mmkkffss..eexxtt44 will create a file system for use with
       ext4, and so on.

       The defaults of the parameters for the newly created  file  system,  if
       not  overridden  by  the  options  listed  below, are controlled by the
       //eettcc//mmkkee22ffss..ccoonnff configuration file.   See  the  mmkkee22ffss..ccoonnff(5)  manual
       page for more details.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --bb _b_l_o_c_k_-_s_i_z_e
              Specify  the  size  of blocks in bytes.  Valid block-size values
              are powers of two from 1024 up to 65536 (however note  that  the
              kernel  is  able  to  mount  only  file  systems with block-size
              smaller or equal to the system page size - 4k on x86 systems, up
              to  64k  on ppc64 or aarch64 depending on kernel configuration).
              If omitted, block-size is heuristically determined by  the  file
              system  size  and the expected usage of the file system (see the
              --TT option).  In most common cases, the default block size is 4k.
              If  _b_l_o_c_k_-_s_i_z_e is preceded by a negative sign ('-'), then mmkkee22ffss
              will use heuristics to determine  the  appropriate  block  size,
              with  the constraint that the block size will be at least _b_l_o_c_k_-
              _s_i_z_e bytes.  This is useful for certain hardware  devices  which
              require that the blocksize be a multiple of 2k.

       --cc     Check the device for bad blocks before creating the file system.
              If this option is specified twice, then a slower read-write test
              is used instead of a fast read-only test.

       --CC  cclluusstteerr--ssiizzee
              Specify  the size of cluster in bytes for file systems using the
              bigalloc feature.  Valid cluster-size values are  from  2048  to
              256M  bytes  per cluster.  This can only be specified if the bi‐
              galloc feature is enabled.  (See the eexxtt44 ((55)) man page for  more
              details  about bigalloc.)   The default cluster size if bigalloc
              is enabled is 16 times the block size.

       --dd _r_o_o_t_-_d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y
              Copy the contents of the given directory into the root directory
              of the file system.

       --DD     Use  direct  I/O  when  writing to the disk.  This avoids mke2fs
              dirtying a lot of buffer cache memory, which  may  impact  other
              applications  running  on a busy server.  This option will cause
              mke2fs to run much more slowly, however, so there is a  tradeoff
              to using direct I/O.

       --ee _e_r_r_o_r_-_b_e_h_a_v_i_o_r
              Change the behavior of the kernel code when errors are detected.
              In all cases, a file system error will cause ee22ffsscckk(8) to  check
              the  file system on the next boot.  _e_r_r_o_r_-_b_e_h_a_v_i_o_r can be one of
              the following:

                   ccoonnttiinnuuee    Continue normal execution.

                   rreemmoouunntt--rroo  Remount file system read-only.

                   ppaanniicc       Cause a kernel panic.

